"── 99_winui_host.mst — WG5b-2: Accept, over image_store::flows ──────────
 docs/ROADMAP.md's WG5 row, its last clause: *Accept persisting to the image
 byte-identically to the web path (the CG8 gate, re-run)*.

 THE GATE IS THE SPECIFICATION. Two GUIs that wrote the image differently
 would be a corruption bug with a user interface in front of it, so nothing
 here reimplements a write. Every one of these calls lands in
 `image_store::flows` — the web GUI's OWN path — through `winui_host.dll`,
 and the error text the transcript shows is `flows`' own words, unedited.

 HOW IT REACHES RUST AT ALL, which was the hard part of this slice and is
 recorded in full in `docs/sprints/sprint_wg5_detail.md`: `image_store`
 depends on the VM, so a primitive would be a dependency cycle; a downstream
 crate cannot add one either (WG2 Δ 1); the Mac gets there through the ObjC
 runtime and Windows has no counterpart. WG5b-2's one core change is the FFI
 pragma's new `library:` part, and this file is its first user.

 THE READ/WRITE SPLIT, inherited from the Mac deliberately (66_cocoabrowser's
 own note): the hierarchy and selector rows in 98 come from the PRIMARY's
 live reflection; the image is what Accept WRITES to. An instance variable
 added here changes shape and so takes effect on the next boot — there is no
 `become:` — which is `flows`' documented behaviour and not a shortcoming of
 this port."

Object subclass: WinHost [
    <classVars: Available>

    "── The bindings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `library:` sends these straight to `resolve_export(Some(..))`, which
     GetModuleHandleA's then LoadLibraryA's the DLL. It deliberately does
     NOT fall back to the system-DLL probe on a miss: a same-named export
     from user32 answering a call meant for the host service is the one
     outcome worse than not resolving at all."

    WinHost class >> ping ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostPing library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #()>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostPing'
    ]

    WinHost class >> rawSaveMethod: cls <Integer> side: side <Integer> source: src <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostSaveMethod library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostSaveMethod'
    ]

    WinHost class >> rawNewClass: src <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostNewClass library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostNewClass'
    ]

    WinHost class >> rawAddVariable: cls <Integer> isClassVar: k <Integer> name: nm <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostAddVariable library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostAddVariable'
    ]

    WinHost class >> rawMethodSource: cls <Integer> side: side <Integer> selector: sel <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostMethodSource library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostMethodSource'
    ]

    WinHost class >> rawLastMessage ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostLastMessage library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #()>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostLastMessage'
    ]

    WinHost class >> rawLastMessageLen ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmHostLastMessageLen library: #'winui_host.dll' ret: #g args: #()>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmHostLastMessageLen'
    ]

    "── Availability, checked once and REMEMBERED ────────────────────────"

    WinHost class >> available ^ <Boolean> [
        "The failure this separates is otherwise silent and baffling. If the
         DLL is not beside the exe, or the name is wrong, every call fails
         identically to a genuine `flows` refusal — `the primitive failed`
         either way — and those are entirely different problems with entirely
         different fixes.

         Cached because a miss costs a LoadLibraryA attempt per call, and
         because the answer cannot change within a run: a DLL that is not
         there when the window opens will not appear later."
        Available isNil ifTrue: [
            Available := [ self ping = 16r5747 ] on: Error do: [ :e | false ] ].
        ^Available
    ]

    WinHost class >> lastMessage ^ <String> [
        "Read by COUNT, not by scanning for a terminator — the host returns
         the length excluding the NUL for exactly this reason."
        | n |
        n := self rawLastMessageLen.
        n <= 0 ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        ^WinArena stringFromUtf16At: self rawLastMessage units: n
    ]

    "── The three writes ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Each answers #(ok message): `ok` a Boolean, `message` the host's own
     text — the saved selector on success, `flows`' refusal on failure. The
     caller decides what to do with it; this class never touches the UI."

    WinHost class >> saveMethod: className <String> side: side <Symbol> source: src <String> ^ <Array> [
        | rc |
        self available ifFalse: [ ^self unavailable ].
        rc := self rawSaveMethod: (WinArena nativeUtf16: className)
                  side: (side = #class ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ])
                  source: (WinArena nativeUtf16: src).
        ^Array with: rc = 0 with: self lastMessage
    ]

    WinHost class >> newClassFrom: src <String> ^ <Array> [
        | rc |
        self available ifFalse: [ ^self unavailable ].
        rc := self rawNewClass: (WinArena nativeUtf16: src).
        ^Array with: rc = 0 with: self lastMessage
    ]

    WinHost class >> addVariableTo: className <String> classVar: isClassVar <Boolean> named: nm <String> ^ <Array> [
        | rc |
        self available ifFalse: [ ^self unavailable ].
        rc := self rawAddVariable: (WinArena nativeUtf16: className)
                  isClassVar: (isClassVar ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ])
                  name: (WinArena nativeUtf16: nm).
        ^Array with: rc = 0 with: self lastMessage
    ]

    WinHost class >> methodSourceFor: className <String> side: side <Symbol> selector: sel <String> ^ <Array> [
        "The READ half, and the reason the browser is a browser rather than a
         list of names. Source is IMAGE data by the same deliberate split the
         Mac uses: names come from the primary's live reflection, text comes
         from the image -- so what is shown is what the next boot will read."
        | rc |
        self available ifFalse: [ ^self unavailable ].
        rc := self rawMethodSource: (WinArena nativeUtf16: className)
                  side: (side = #class ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ])
                  selector: (WinArena nativeUtf16: sel).
        ^Array with: rc = 0 with: self lastMessage
    ]

    WinHost class >> unavailable ^ <Array> [
        "Named rather than inlined three times because the wording IS the
         diagnosis: it tells whoever hits it where the DLL is meant to be."
        ^Array with: false with: 'The host service is not available — winui_host.dll must sit beside the executable.'
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: AcceptCount>

    "── Accept ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> acceptSource [
        "The browser's Accept, and the shape of it is the Mac's: write the
         IMAGE first, then live-compile on the PRIMARY.

         That order is deliberate and worth stating. The image is the record
         — it is what the next boot reads — so a write that fails must stop
         here and say so, rather than leaving a class live in one VM and
         absent from the file. Live-compiling first would make the browser
         look right and the image wrong, which is the failure a user does not
         discover until tomorrow."
        ^self acceptSourceText: self sourcePaneText
    ]

    WinShell class >> acceptSourceText: source <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Everything Accept DOES, with the one control read lifted out.

         Split for the same reason the Mac's browser has scripting helpers
         that double as its jump path: the gate can then drive the real write
         — real FFI, real DLL, real `flows` — with no window in the way, and
         what it exercises is the SAME method the button reaches rather than a
         parallel one written for testing. A gate that drove its own private
         copy of this would prove only that the copy worked."
        | cls r |
        cls := self browserSelectedClass.
        (cls isNil or: [ source isEmpty ]) ifTrue: [
            self appendTranscript: 'Accept: select a class and enter a method first.'.
            ^false ].
        r := WinHost saveMethod: cls side: self browserSelectedSide source: source.
        (r at: 1) ifFalse: [
            self appendTranscript: 'Accept refused: ' , (r at: 2).
            ^false ].
        AcceptCount := (AcceptCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ AcceptCount ]) + 1.
        self appendTranscript: 'Accepted ' , cls , ' >> ' , (r at: 2) , ' (image)'.
        self liveCompile: source into: cls.
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> liveCompile: source <String> into: cls <String> [
        "The second half: make it live in the PRIMARY, where the objects are.
         Over the ordinary `uiDoit:` channel — the same seam WG4 D1 built and
         WG5a's Do It already uses — because a compile is just evaluation and
         inventing a second channel for it would be two things to keep
         correct instead of one.

         Reopening the class is how a method is added to an EXISTING class
         from source: `flows::reopen_one_method` builds exactly this text for
         the web path, and using the same shape keeps the two GUIs compiling
         identically."
        | text |
        text := cls , ' extend [ ' , source , ' ]'.
        "BEST-EFFORT, and deliberately so. The image write above already
         succeeded and is the RECORD — it is what the next boot reads. If the
         primary is gone (it can die, and in a CLI run there is none at all)
         the right outcome is a saved method and an honest note, not an
         exception that unwinds a completed write and leaves the user
         believing nothing happened.

         Found by the gate: driving `acceptSourceText:` headlessly took the
         whole run down with `unknown or dead worker` AFTER the image had been
         written correctly — which is exactly the failure this guard is for,
         arriving from the direction that makes it obvious."
        [ Worker uiDoit: text onReplyTimed: [ :result :ms |
            WinShell appendTranscript: 'Compiled into the primary (' , ms printString , ' ms)' ] ]
            on: Error
            do: [ :e |
                self appendTranscript: 'Saved to the image; not compiled live (' , e messageText , ')' ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> acceptCount ^ <Integer> [ ^AcceptCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ AcceptCount ] ]

    WinShell class >> sourceTextFor: cls <Object> selector: sel <Object> ^ <String> [
        "Split out from `updateSourcePane` so the decision is testable without
         a window — the pane's job is then only to display what this answers."
        | nl r |
        cls isNil ifTrue: [ ^'Select a class.' ].
        nl := String with: Character cr with: Character nl.
        sel isNil ifTrue: [
            ^cls , nl , nl ,
             'variables: ' , (self browserVariables inject: '' into: [ :a :b | a , b , ' ' ]) , nl , nl ,
             'Select a selector to see its source, or type a method here and press Accept.' ].
        r := WinHost methodSourceFor: cls side: self browserSelectedSide selector: sel.
        (r at: 1) ifFalse: [ ^cls , ' >> ' , sel , nl , nl , (r at: 2) ].
        (r at: 2) isEmpty ifTrue: [
            ^cls , ' >> ' , sel , nl , nl ,
             'No source in the image for this selector yet — it exists live but has not been Accepted.' ].
        ^self crlf: (r at: 2)
    ]

    WinShell class >> crlf: aString <String> ^ <String> [
        "A Win32 EDIT wants CRLF and the image stores LF, so a method's real
         source arrived as one long line with a box at every break — the whole
         body on line 1, which reads as a browser that cannot show source
         rather than one showing it in the wrong line endings.

         Converted HERE, at the boundary, rather than in the image: LF is what
         the image should hold (it is what every .mst file holds and what the
         web GUI reads), and a control's display convention is the control's
         business. The same rule the sprint already states for EM_GETSEL's
         UTF-16 offsets — convert at the boundary, exactly once."
        | out |
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        aString do: [ :c |
            c = Character nl
                ifTrue: [ out nextPut: Character cr. out nextPut: Character nl ]
                ifFalse: [ c = Character cr ifFalse: [ out nextPut: c ] ] ].
        ^out contents
    ]

    WinShell class >> sourcePaneText ^ <String> [
        "`browserSelectedSide` is NOT defined here on purpose. 98 already has
         it, reading the `BrowserSide` the protocols pane sets -- and because
         this file loads last, a second definition would silently WIN and
         ignore whichever side the user actually clicked. Reopening a class
         across files makes that shadowing invisible, so it is worth a note
         rather than a rediscovery."
        ^(self controlNamed: #browserSource) ifNil: [ '' ] ifNotNil: [ :c | c text ]
    ]
]
